SQLite can support vector database capabilities, but it requires a specialized extension because native SQLite does not include 
vector types or similarity search out of the box.

By default, SQLite only supports data types like integers, text, and blobs.  To handle vector embeddings, you must load an external 
extension that introduces a native vector data type, high-performance distance functions (like Cosine or L2 distance), and index 
acceleration structures like Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW).


The Two Best Extensions for SQLite Vector Search:
************************************************

Because SQLite is modular, developers have created powerful extensions specifically designed for local Retrieval-Augmented Generation 
(RAG) pipelines:

|Extension Name | Primary Developer / Backer             | Key Features & Strengths                | Best For                                  |
|---------------|----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|
|sqlite-vec     | Alex Garcia (independent / open-source)| Written in pure C; extremely lightweight| Minimalist setups,mobile apps,edge devices|
|               |                                        | zero dependencies; highly portable      |                                           |
|               |                                        |                                         |                                           |
|sqlite-vss     | Alex Garcia / Powered by Faiss         | Utilizes Facebook's powerful FAISS      | Larger local datasets requiring advanced  |
|               |                                        | library; highly performant on massive   | clustering                                |
|               |                                        | datasets.                               |                                           |
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


- Flat/Linear Search: SQLite scans every single row one by one. This is fast for 1,000 items, but becomes painfully slow as you reach 
  tens of thousands of rows.
- Indexed Search (HNSW): The extension builds a navigational graph structure. Searching takes a tiny, nearly flat fraction of a second, 
  regardless of how many vectors you add.


sqlite-vec is a lightweight, dependency-free SQLite extension written in C that enables vector search. It lets you store and run 
similarity queries on float, int8, and binary vectors directly inside local SQLite databases across mobile devices, laptops, and 
browsers.

Core capabilities and technical details of sqlite-vec:
 - Virtual Tables (vec0): Stores vector embeddings and handles nearest-neighbor constraints using standard SQL syntax via MATCH operators.
 - Supported Distance Metrics: Computes L2 (Euclidean), L1 (Manhattan), cosine similarity, and Hamming distance.
 - Hardware Acceleration: Uses low-level SIMD intrinsics (AVX on x86 and NEON on ARM) to speed up distance computations.
 - Cross-Platform: Runs wherever SQLite runs, including Linux, macOS, Windows, Raspberry Pis, and WebAssembly (WASM) in browsers.
 - Language Bindings: Provides easy integration wrappers for Python, Node.js, Rust, Go, and Ruby




Ex Table creation, Insert, Vector match:
***************************************
CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE vec_articles USING vec_each  <- deviation from standard
	core deviation from standard by sev support add is  VIRTUAL and USING.

	When you use those keywords, you are telling SQLite: "Do not use your normal, built-in storage engine for this table. Instead, 
	hand control over to an external extension module."Here is the exact breakdown of how a standard table creation differs from a 
	virtual table creation:

INSERT INTO articles (id, title, content, embedding) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)", (article_id, title, content, blob_vector) <- syntax as standard
	but deviation is; 
  		- Virtual Table (sqlite-vec): SQLite intercepts the INSERT command. It hands the binary BLOB vector over to the sqlite-vec 
		  extension, 
    		  which instantly runs the math to place that vector into its specialized spatial index graph.
  		- sqlite-vec converts your Python float array into a raw binary BLOB of 4-byte floating-point numbers.
  
SELECT ... WHERE v.headline_embedding MATCH ? AND v.k = 1 <- deviation from standard
	uses a syntax trick unique to SQLite extensions,.
	1. MATCH ? (The Distance Calculator)
		In standard SQL, you use = to find exact matches or LIKE to find matching text.
		But with vectors, you never look for an exact match. You look for the closest vector using geometry.
			- The Deviation: sqlite-vec overrides the standard SQL MATCH keyword.
			- What it does: Instead of looking for a matching string, MATCH ? tells the database engine: "Take the user's 
			  query vector (the ?), calculate the mathematical distance to every vector in the table, and rank them from 
			  closest to furthest.
	2. AND v.k = 1 (The Limit / Neighbor Count)
		In normal SQL, if you only want the top result, you put LIMIT 1 at the very end of your query.
		With virtual vector tables, you must use k = 1 inside the WHERE clause instead.
			- The "K" Parameter: In data science, "K" stands for K-Nearest Neighbors.
			- Why it is in the WHERE clause: The underlying C-code of sqlite-vec needs to know before it starts searching 
			  how many close items you want. Passing v.k = 1 forces the internal index search algorithm to stop immediately 
			  after finding the single best match, saving a massive amount of CPU cycles.
			



Structural Comparison:
*********************

|Feature       |  Standard Table Syntax | Virtual Table Syntax                                 |
|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Keywords      | CREATE TABLE           | CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE                                 |
|Engine        | SQLite Native B-Tree   | External Module (e.g., vec_each)                     |
|Configuration | Explicit column types  | Module-specific arguments (like USING vec_each(...)) |
|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|


